WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.611
Architecture modeling makes selected decisions
explicit.

00:00:03.611 --> 00:00:09.672
Start with the question, then choose components,
relationships, constraints and rationale that

00:00:09.672 --> 00:00:10.317
answer it.

00:00:10.317 --> 00:00:12.897
More detail is not automatically better.

00:00:12.897 --> 00:00:19.151
The model must remain understandable and
maintainable, while preserving the assumptions
needed to

00:00:19.151 --> 00:00:21.086
evaluate behavior and quality.

00:00:21.086 --> 00:00:26.567
Treat runtime state and structural organization as
related but distinct perspectives.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:31.227
Use early models to challenge a design while change
is still affordable.

00:00:31.227 --> 00:00:37.654
An execution graph estimates required work without
contention, making it useful for spotting obvious

00:00:37.654 --> 00:00:38.233
problems.

00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:41.703
A good baseline does not establish loaded
performance.

00:00:41.703 --> 00:00:47.423
Refine assumptions with prototypes and measurements,
then add queueing or simulation when

00:00:47.423 --> 00:00:49.608
competition for resources matters.

00:00:49.608 --> 00:00:54.846
An execution graph describes sequencing, repetition,
alternatives and concurrency.

00:00:54.846 --> 00:00:58.806
Reduce each structure using the metric you intend to
estimate.

00:00:58.806 --> 00:01:04.810
A probability-weighted case answers an average-path
question, while minimum and maximum answer

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:06.088
different questions.

00:01:06.088 --> 00:01:12.347
Keep parallel work and asynchronous work distinct,
and remember that a no-contention graph has not

00:01:12.347 --> 00:01:15.413
yet accounted for waiting behind other requests.
